#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53eff04f-9cf8-5a15-bf1e-3202677f56a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236352} {:answer/id #uuid "8deed6bb-9a7d-5a1d-97f3-9313fbc9e6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236715} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c66e0e-d6d3-51c4-80a3-331232d651f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236822} {:answer/id #uuid "f249a950-eb52-5a20-b5ef-cc54b4326e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237345} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c62bc3-8050-5b88-89ef-e157af90b11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8deed6bb-9a7d-5a1d-97f3-9313fbc9e6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236715} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9b86e15-3d6e-5aa9-80bd-3e76375a1e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4df6773c-7996-5e96-920f-edaf9d38b1f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-101310" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3-y+5z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=26\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,-1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5 &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=26$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =26 \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ \\frac{26}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=∓1\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(0,-1,5),\\,(0,1,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,5)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,-5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,29\\,$ | $\\,-23\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-23.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dc044b-24e6-5b1f-8787-d56ca91a9176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278646} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ba600-5352-5861-a7a7-22e72871086b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279690} {:answer/id #uuid "7e357422-bf28-5f1c-8a6c-e23d12be25da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279725} {:answer/id #uuid "b410e16c-dfd0-5424-90b9-d8368e817d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280177} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f2419e-a59e-59f5-8bb8-6f6008d18dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ba600-5352-5861-a7a7-22e72871086b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279690} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd67f28a-cbb2-587d-9c14-b9a7a306bc81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15a21e57-2278-5487-9586-a1ff33b69b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-162767" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y+4z$ on the ellipsoid $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=21\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=8\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ 4 &=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{8\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=21$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =21 \\\\ 4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =21 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{21}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=\\pm 2,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},2,2),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{21}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f2ed59-53c9-5b2f-b846-8683f58bc76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279243} {:answer/id #uuid "babeccb9-a237-5cb0-82bc-c21e269e85fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280238} {:answer/id #uuid "c343408b-3b86-5652-aeee-97da743613e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280307} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1dcaea-8a35-5594-a4c9-8d44778d1d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280383} {:answer/id #uuid "ff19c910-670d-574b-b14d-f52081305537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "babeccb9-a237-5cb0-82bc-c21e269e85fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280238} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fe8166-4c89-5f92-bcbe-bb29871f25b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "301d5a0d-2c92-53f7-94cf-b2627d84c6ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-162768" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1-2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y &=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1+2\\lambda ) &=1 \\\\ y(1-4\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1+\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}),(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(-1,0)$ and its minimum value is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44296748-1b1f-55fb-b6be-3f1f679a363f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279202} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c0be99-daac-5fe1-aa90-0211c18e781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280227} {:answer/id #uuid "c76ad7f1-b80d-5a93-af5f-ef37cbad1d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280339} {:answer/id #uuid "e72548ca-744f-5dba-81cb-314ca4deed0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280627} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2e7d8a-6624-5d7a-bde3-c06967f94904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff2e7d8a-6624-5d7a-bde3-c06967f94904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280823} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ccfe924-d83d-5c3b-b4d2-db813f0c0a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "046d11e3-1904-5ae7-b876-315526818d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162770" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${y}^{2}-4x=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-4x=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-4,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=-4\\lambda \\\\ -4y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2\\lambda \\\\ y(\\lambda +2) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-2(-2)\\,⇒\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(4,-4),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,\\pm 4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,\\pm 4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(4,\\pm 4)$ and its minimum value is $-16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba6dc59a-d58e-52fa-ab73-eb12adb3b9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123255} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be39ec-ebe1-5490-8c20-efd23eff06dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123256} {:answer/id #uuid "0469e86f-4896-589f-be98-7d2dcb9b622a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123257} {:answer/id #uuid "66fdae75-3fd9-5769-80cc-77dba87e1ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123258} {:answer/id #uuid "72fceb0d-f6b0-5e5c-9ecb-2fc5b7e2e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0469e86f-4896-589f-be98-7d2dcb9b622a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123257} :answer-field/id #uuid "53789dcc-b6e4-5a59-b807-fa2867d7d6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4948ed57-4d3d-575d-8636-517fc5219397" :question/math-academy-id "q-37869" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=13\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=13$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,-3),\\,(2,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,-3)$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-13$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,3),$ and its maximum value is $13.$"}]}
